Things to do in Amboise? Far more than the royal château, and that is what the guidebooks forget to say. We live ten minutes from town, on the other bank of the Loire, and our guests ask us the question every morning at breakfast. Here are our 12 ideas, in the order we recommend them, with what is worth the detour and what is not.

## What can you do in Amboise in one day?

For one day in Amboise, see the royal château in the morning, the Clos Lucé in the afternoon and the Loire riverbanks at sunset, entirely on foot. That order avoids both the queues and the back-and-forth.

### 1. The royal château, at opening time

The royal Château d'Amboise towers over the town and the river, and it is open all year. Charles VIII and François I lived here, and Leonardo da Vinci is buried in its Saint-Hubert chapel, perched on the rampart. Allow an hour and a half. Arrive at opening time: by 11 am in summer, groups arrive by the coachload and the terraces fill up.

### 2. The Clos Lucé, in the afternoon

A few hundred metres away, the Clos Lucé is the house where Leonardo spent his last three years, from 1516 to 1519. The house takes an hour, but it is the park, with full-size models of his machines, that keeps families there until closing time. Allow two to three hours in all.

### 3. The quays and the Île d'Or, in the early evening

Cross the bridge to the Île d'Or as the light drops. From there, and from the north bank, you see the royal château along its full length, with the Loire in the foreground. Our view: it is the best moment of the day in Amboise, and it is free.

## What are the free things to do in Amboise?

The best free things to do in Amboise are the Sunday market, the Île d'Or, the Rue Nationale with its Clock Tower, and the Loire riverbanks. You can fill a whole day without paying a single entrance fee.

### 4. The Rue Nationale and the Clock Tower

The pedestrian street runs through the old town beneath the Tour de l'Horloge, a 15th-century belfry. Look up above the souvenir shops: the half-timbered façades are worth more than the shop windows. Below the château, on the quay, do not miss the fountain designed by Max Ernst, which many visitors take for an ordinary sculpture.

### 5. The Île d'Or, on foot or by bike

The Île d'Or splits the Loire in two opposite the château. You walk along the water, between willows and sandbanks, with no cars. On summer evenings, this is where Amboise locals come to cool off, and the Loire à Vélo route runs close by if you want to go further: we cover the routes in our guide to [Loire Valley cycling from Amboise](/en/blog/loire-valley-cycling-amboise).

![The Château de la Huberdière reflected in the lake in its grounds, in autumn, under a dappled sky](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-13.webp "Our lake in autumn. The grounds are a short walk from the bedrooms, and the centre of Amboise is ten minutes by car, across the Loire.")

## The Amboise Sunday market: what time should you go?

The big Amboise market is held on Sunday mornings on the Quai du Général-de-Gaulle, beside the Loire, and it is worth being there before 10 am. A second, smaller market takes place on Friday mornings.

### 6. The Sunday market, before 10 am

It is one of the largest markets in Touraine, stretching for hundreds of metres along the river. Sainte-Maure goat's cheese, rillettes, Touraine strawberries in season, Vouvray and Montlouis wines: everything the region produces passes through. From 11 am you shuffle along and parking becomes impossible. Our advice: come early, stock up for a picnic, and leave before the crowds.

## Where can you walk in and around Amboise?

For walks in Amboise, alternate between the town on foot, the Loire riverbanks and two green spaces a few minutes away: the Pagode de Chanteloup and the Amboise forest. The town itself can be seen entirely on foot in half a day.

### 7. The Pagode de Chanteloup

A few minutes south of town, the Pagode de Chanteloup is all that remains of the Duc de Choiseul's château: an 18th-century Chinese-style tower, 44 metres tall. Climb to the top for the view over the valley and the forest, then stay in the park: old wooden games, rowing boats on the lake, a picnic on the grass. It closes for part of the winter, so check the dates before you go.

### 8. The Amboise forest

The Amboise forest begins south of town and stretches towards Chenonceau. Its straight rides are made for walking or cycling, and it is the only walk in the area that stays in the shade in July.

## What to do in Amboise with children, or when it rains?

With children in Amboise, the Clos Lucé park, the Pagode de Chanteloup and the Mini-Châteaux park keep them busy; when it rains, head for the cellars and the aquarium. There is enough to fill a wet day without boredom.

### 9. The Mini-Châteaux park and the aquarium

The Parc des Mini-Châteaux, just outside town, shows the Loire châteaux as scale models. The freshwater aquarium in Lussault-sur-Loire, ten minutes to the west, devoted to the fish of the Loire and its rivers, is our favourite fallback on rainy days.

### 10. The tufa cellars

Cellars dug into the white stone stay cool all year, at roughly twelve degrees, whether it is pouring or 35 °C outside. In Amboise itself, the Caves Ambacia offer visits with tastings. Up on the hillside, Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire have dozens more, and the smell of damp stone hits you as soon as you step inside.

![The château's front steps and stone staircase under a blue sky, framed by foliage](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-26.webp "The château's façade, on the right bank of the Loire. Families stay in 10 bedrooms, up to 21 people when the whole estate is booked for exclusive use.")

For a large group, the simplest option is often to [rent a château for a weekend with friends or family](/en/blog/rent-chateau-group-weekend): everyone explores at their own pace by day and the whole party meets up in the evening.

## What to do in Amboise in the evening, and where to eat?

In the evening, Amboise comes alive on its quays and around the royal château, where most of the restaurants are; always book on a Saturday, as the town fills up quickly. On weekdays and out of season, many kitchens close early.

### 11. Dinner below the château, or stay on the estate

The streets running down from the royal château to the Loire hold most of the restaurants, from bistros to more ambitious tables. Out of season, check opening days: in November, several places close on Sunday evening and Monday. We recommend booking before you leave the hotel rather than trying your luck at 8.30 pm.

Our guests often have another option. From 1 May to 30 September, we serve dinner to residents every evening except Sunday, with large sharing boards, and on Thursday evenings dinner is entirely Italian. The rest of the year, the table opens on request for groups of six or more. Everything is explained on our [château dining](/en/chateau-dining-touraine) page, and in detail in our article on [the table d'hôtes in Touraine](/en/blog/table-dhotes-loire-chateau).

![A red door in the tufa façade, framed by ivy, with purple salvias and pink cosmos in the foreground](/images/inline/chateau-exterieur/chateau-exterieur-03.webp "The garden along the façade, in summer. Dinner is served outdoors from May to September, every evening except Sunday.")

## What to do around Amboise?

Around Amboise, Chenonceau is 20 minutes away, Chaumont-sur-Loire 25, the Vouvray cellars a quarter of an hour and Beauval Zoo roughly three quarters of an hour. Amboise is the best base in the valley, because it sits right in the middle.

### 12. Neighbouring villages and châteaux

Chenonceau first, at opening time with a ticket bought the day before. Then Chaumont-sur-Loire, for its garden festival from April to November. Then two villages the guidebooks rarely mention: Chédigny, the only village in France listed as a Jardin remarquable, covered in roses in May and June, and Montrésor, listed among the most beautiful villages of France. To choose between the great châteaux, our guide to [visiting the Loire châteaux](/en/blog/visiting-loire-chateaux) gives the order we recommend.

A useful piece of context: the Loire Valley between Sully-sur-Loire and Chalonnes has been a UNESCO World Heritage Site since 2000, as a cultural landscape. It is the river, the hillsides and the villages that are protected, not just the châteaux: a day without a monument is never a wasted day here.

## What we do not recommend

Do not try the royal château, the Clos Lucé and Chenonceau on the same day: it works on paper and is exhausting in practice, especially with children. Avoid the Rue Nationale at 3 pm on an August Saturday too, unless you enjoy queuing outside ice-cream shops. And do not drive to the Sunday market after 10.30 am.

On the other hand, November is a season we will happily defend. The châteaux stay open, the queues have gone, and the estate's grounds take on their autumn colours.

## Where to stay to visit Amboise?

To visit Amboise, stay less than fifteen minutes from the centre: on the north bank if you want calm and a view of the river, in the centre if you want to do everything on foot. We are in the first category.

The Château de la Huberdière is a charming 3-star hotel in Nazelles-Négron, ten minutes from Amboise and 2 hrs 20 from Paris, in 14 hectares of enclosed grounds. We have 10 bedrooms and a heated pool from April to October. We do not hire out bikes, but we will point you to the nearest rental shop before you arrive. For other ways to enjoy the region, our article on [sleeping in a Loire château](/en/blog/sleep-in-a-loire-chateau) compares the options.

If Amboise tempts you for a weekend, discover our bedrooms and [book your stay near Amboise](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise): we reply to every enquiry within 24 hours.
